WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.277 --> 00:00:08.158
Imagine that you discovered something very important.

2
00:00:08.819 --> 00:00:13.860
Imagine that your discovery was so important that it would change the way people think and live.

3
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.201
I'm sure that you would run out and tell everyone about your new discovery.

4
00:00:18.902 --> 00:00:21.682
You might write a book about it and talk to many people.

5
00:00:23.063 --> 00:00:26.984
Now imagine that no one believes you, and they even get angry with you.

6
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.023
They accuse you of being a liar and tell you that you are evil.

7
00:00:31.703 --> 00:00:37.007
Finally, because you keep insisting that you are right, they throw you into prison for the rest of

8
00:00:37.047 --> 00:00:37.527
your life.

9
00:00:40.209 --> 00:00:43.011
That is exactly what happened to Galileo Galilei.

10
00:00:43.612 --> 00:00:48.295
Galileo was born in Pisa, which is a city in Italy, in 1564.

11
00:00:49.096 --> 00:00:54.580
Galileo came from a well-respected family, and his parents expected him to become a doctor.

12
00:00:56.136 --> 00:01:01.459
However, much to his father's disappointment, Galileo decided not to be a doctor.

13
00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:04.941
He was more interested in mathematics and philosophy.

14
00:01:05.741 --> 00:01:11.564
During his early years as a professor of mathematics, Galileo made some very important discoveries.

15
00:01:13.085 --> 00:01:18.248
For instance, if you dropped a heavy ball and a light ball, which would hit the ground first?

16
00:01:19.376 --> 00:01:22.618
Scientists used to think that the heavy ball would land first.

17
00:01:23.098 --> 00:01:28.501
But Galileo believed they would hit the ground at the same time, and he wrote a mathematical formula

18
00:01:28.561 --> 00:01:29.862
to explain his idea.

19
00:01:32.864 --> 00:01:37.727
To prove his theory, he climbed to the top of the famous Leaning Tower of Pisa and dropped

20
00:01:37.787 --> 00:01:41.229
two balls of the same size, one heavy and one light.

21
00:01:42.049 --> 00:01:43.590
He proved his theory was correct.

22
00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.592
The two balls landed at the exact same time.

23
00:01:47.736 --> 00:01:52.660
Because of this experiment, most people believed Galileo and accepted his new theory.

24
00:01:53.641 --> 00:01:59.446
However, some people were not happy because Galileo's new ideas contradicted the old ones.

25
00:02:00.227 --> 00:02:03.990
Sometimes new ideas make people feel uncomfortable and afraid.

26
00:02:05.551 --> 00:02:11.636
Galileo's life took a drastic turn in 1609, when he heard about a new and incredible invention from

27
00:02:11.656 --> 00:02:12.317
the Netherlands.

28
00:02:13.277 --> 00:02:15.980
It was a tool that made distant objects look near.

29
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.143
It was the telescope, and Galileo thought it was a fantastic invention.

30
00:02:22.784 --> 00:02:26.387
Galileo began working on the telescope to make it more powerful.

31
00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:31.630
He taught himself how to grind glass in order to make lenses for his telescope.

32
00:02:33.271 --> 00:02:38.014
After many years and a lot of hard work, he made a telescope so powerful that it could

33
00:02:38.095 --> 00:02:40.096
magnify the stars and planets.

34
00:02:42.618 --> 00:02:45.800
Before this, people could only view stars with their eyes.

35
00:02:46.524 --> 00:02:52.168
Galileo's telescope impressed many people, and he became the highest-paid professor at his university.

36
00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:56.971
Over the next few months, he made even more powerful telescopes.

37
00:02:57.751 --> 00:03:00.453
This was when Galileo's real problems started.

38
00:03:01.954 --> 00:03:06.257
At first he discovered some very interesting and wonderful things that no one questioned.

39
00:03:06.857 --> 00:03:10.980
For example, he found that the moon was very bumpy instead of smooth.

40
00:03:11.916 --> 00:03:16.998
He also discovered that Jupiter had four moons and that there were many more stars than people could

41
00:03:17.058 --> 00:03:18.398
see with the naked eye.

42
00:03:20.119 --> 00:03:24.340
However, Galileo soon discovered something that made many people very angry.

43
00:03:25.461 --> 00:03:31.103
By looking into space and observing the planets, the sun, and the stars, he discovered that Earth was

44
00:03:31.123 --> 00:03:34.524
a ball that revolved around the sun like the other planets.

45
00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:42.304
Many religious leaders thought that Galileo didn't believe in God and was questioning how the world was created.

46
00:03:43.305 --> 00:03:45.766
They accused Galileo of doubting God's Word.

47
00:03:46.827 --> 00:03:53.231
This was a big problem for Galileo because at that time, the Catholic Church was the most powerful

48
00:03:53.311 --> 00:03:54.652
organization in the world.

49
00:03:58.134 --> 00:04:01.376
The religious leaders brought Galileo before the Inquisition.

50
00:04:02.192 --> 00:04:08.017
The Inquisition was a religious court that decided if a person was saying things against the Christian faith.

51
00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:15.443
If the judges decided that a person was speaking against God, they decided how to punish him.

52
00:04:16.704 --> 00:04:22.690
The Inquisition decided that Galileo was against God, and his punishment was that he should not speak or

53
00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.711
write about his discoveries anymore.

54
00:04:26.172 --> 00:04:30.116
But Galileo was a true scientist, and he did not want to be quiet.

55
00:04:31.444 --> 00:04:36.829
So he continued to argue that his discoveries were not against the Bible and not against God.

56
00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.811
He wanted people to hear what he was saying.

57
00:04:41.193 --> 00:04:44.395
Once again Galileo was called before the Inquisition.

58
00:04:45.857 --> 00:04:50.661
Now he was in very big trouble because this time he had disobeyed the Inquisition.

59
00:04:51.181 --> 00:04:57.546
In order to stop Galileo from spreading his lies to other people, the Inquisition arrested him and imprisoned

60
00:04:57.587 --> 00:04:59.028
him for the rest of his life.

61
00:05:00.156 --> 00:05:05.398
Of course, today we know that Galileo was right about many of his theories, and he has become

62
00:05:05.518 --> 00:05:07.959
one of the most respected thinkers of all time.

63
00:05:08.799 --> 00:05:12.061
But at that time, he was the great man who no one believed.
